# CODEOWNERS — review routing for the debugging-generator monorepo
#
# GitHub reads this file to request reviews from the owners of the paths a pull
# request changes. It is also the repository's area map: each block below names
# one area of the monorepo and says why a change there deserves its reviewer.
# What each area is and how the areas fit together is described in
# docs/ARCHITECTURE.md (sections 2 and 3).
#
# OWNERS. Every rule names the repository owner, @ConnerArdman, so every area
# currently routes its reviews to that one account. GitHub honours an owner
# only when it has write access to the repository; the repository owner has
# admin access, which includes write.
# To spread the review load:
#   - replace the owner PER AREA, not with one global search-and-replace, so
#     each block routes to the people who own that area;
#   - name a GitHub user handle or an organisation team (organisation/team
#     form), and that user or team must have write access to the repository,
#     or GitHub ignores the rule.
# To enforce the reviews, enable "Require review from Code Owners" in the
# branch protection rule or ruleset for the protected branches; without it
# reviews are requested but not enforced.
# Never use an email address as an owner: every tracked file, this one
# included, is scanned by `pnpm check:secrets`, and review routing needs only
# handles.
#
# SYNTAX RULES THAT DECIDE THE ORDER OF THIS FILE
#   - The LAST matching pattern wins. Rules therefore run from the broadest to
#     the most specific; a specific rule placed above a broader one is dead.
#   - Patterns follow gitignore syntax, except that negation and bracketed
#     character ranges are not supported, so neither is used here.
#   - A leading slash anchors a pattern to the repository root, so no rule can
#     match a same-named folder nested inside a workspace. A trailing slash
#     names a whole directory; a path without one names a single file.
#   - GitHub uses only the first CODEOWNERS file it finds (.github/, the root,
#     docs/). This is the only one; do not add a second copy elsewhere.
#
# Nothing in this file names the demo application's components, services,
# hooks or route paths: the demo's internals are documented only in
# docs/DEMO.md.

# Catch-all: every path not matched by a more specific rule below, including
# the workspace editor settings under .vscode/.
*                                           @ConnerArdman

